ability simplex} sum(g) [subject to max_k (a0*k + g*k* * c) <= M1]\n\n    But note, this is not standard.\n\n    Actually, we can mimic the previous algorithm's phase:\n\n      Start from `a0` (normalized sequence) and set `M = max(convolve(a0, mode='full'))`.\n\n      Then, solve an LP: max_{g with components in [-M, M]} such that the entire self-convolution sequence of `a0` and `g` has an inf norm bounded by `M`.\n\n    Wait, the standard mirror descent for simplex (as in the previous program) is:\n\n      - Approximate the minimization of the inf norm of the convolution by letting `g` be a probability distribution (so components of `g` are nonnegative) on the lower dimensional space of coefficients.\n\n    The previous program uses iterative updates with a weighted subgradient.\n\n    But our new idea is to ensure progress by restarting when no improvement occurs.\n\n    We will use the same `mirror_descent` function but with adaptive restarts.\n\n    The key difference: the initial sequence and the intermediate steps may be found by smaller-scale optimizations or by generating diverse candidate initializations.\n\n 3. Implementation procedure:\n\n    We will write a function `optimal_step_function()` that:\n\n    a. Initializes a sequence (either from the parent or a Gaussian distribution).\n    b. Sets up a mirror descent algorithm with adaptive step size `L2` and tolerance `M`.\n    c. Keeps track of the best sequence and score.\n    d. After each major update, evaluates the sequence and updates the best score.\n\n    The mirror descent algorithm is as follows:\n\n      import numpy as np\n\n      def mirror_descent(initial_sequence, time_limit, max_iterations):\n\n        n = len(initial_sequence)\n        sequence = initial_sequence / np.sum(initial_sequence)\n\n        current_M = float(evaluate_sequence(sequence.tolist()))\n        best_sequence = sequence.copy()\n        best_M = current_M\n\n        # We'lldo a fast mirror descent with restarting if not improving\n        timer = time.time()\n        no_improvement = 0\n        while time_limit - (time.time() - timer) > 0 and no_improved_value < max_iterations:\n\n          # Project to the simplex\n          sequence = project_simplex(sequence)\n\n          # Compute the gradient: let's use the same compute_subgradient as before\n          conv = fast_convolve(sequence)\n          M_val = conv.max()\n\n          # If the improvement is less than 1.0e-14, we break out? Or continue to improve?\n          if M_val >= 1.2 * best_M:\n            # This is not an improvement; we set a new tolerance\n            tolerance = 1.1 * M_val\n            # Solve the LP with the new tolerance\n            # ... This is just an idea\n            pass\n\n          # else, success: record best and try different initial conditions\n\n    But this is too abstract.\n\n    Given the time and performance constraints, we'll use aabalgorithm and its variations from the previous program, but with a focus on diversity.\n\n    We'll do:\n\n      Run the parent's `mirror_descent` and `rectilinear_flow` functions on initially generated sequences, and then periodically review whether to restart in a different direction.\n\n    We'll expose the following control flow:\n\n      The search will be responsiveto the condition that the best candidate might not improve and then switch to a different area.\n\n    We are allowed up to 1800 seconds, so we will use time allocation across several diversification techniques.\n\n 4. Search choices and useful variations:\n\n    We will use a cyclomatic method that toggles between several optimization techniques and initialization strategies, and this will be triggered by lack of improvement.\n\n    The default will be to run the mirror descent with adaptive hyperparameter tuning, then if progress stalls, trigger a rectangular move or restart with a new perspective.\n\n    Larger sequences with 1800 elements might provide a better attack surface, but we are not required to explore different lengths per s\u00e9.\n\n    We will keep n fixed as 768 (the original length from the parent) to keep the implementation simple.\n\n    We will try to include the following:\n\n      1. Diversification of the initialization function (try bell-shaped instead of Gaussian)\n      2. Use simplex projection but with a different normalization in the objective.\n\n    But note: the objective in evaluate_sequence has sum(a) normalization. We must only work with sequences that are non-negative.\n\n 5. Computational budget and robustness:\n\n    We are allocated 1800 seconds. We'll set a few minutes for each optimization stage, and then combine them sequentially.\n\n    We must design the algorithm to periodically save the best score so far and to not let the score degrade too much.\n\n    If no improvement occurs after a certain number of iterations (say 100), trigger a restart with the symmetric version or with a Gaussian initialization.\n\n    But note: the previous code also has peak reduction and fine CD steps, which may help.\n\n 6. Validation and return contract:\n\n    We will evaluate the final sequence with `evaluate_sequence` and ensure it's not worse than the parent's solution.\n\n    We'll maintain a record of the best sequence and its score, and return it.\n\n    The function must be able to run for at least 1800 seconds or find a solution earlier.",
